In matlab if I am not happy with things, I can clear alll; clear func
then restart the script within matlab itself. Instead of quitting and
restarting. How o I do that in python?

Python (and its ecosystem) doesn't have a true equivalent to clear all
that reliably wipes everything and gives you a clean slate — this
trips up a lot of people coming from Matlab, so it's a fair thing to
ask about. The honest answer depends on what you're running in.

If you're in a plain Python script (.py file) run from terminal:
  There's no "restart in place." The normal workflow is: edit the file,
  then rerun it — python my_script.py — which starts a fresh interpreter
  process each time, so you get a genuinely clean slate
  automatically. This is actually closer to Matlab's clear all in spirit
  than anything you'd do inside a running session, since a full new
  process guarantees no leftover state.

If you're in Jupyter/IPython (notebook or console) — which is probably
the closer match to how you use Matlab interactively:

 %reset — clears all variables in the namespace, closest analog to
  clear all. It'll ask "Once deleted, variables cannot be
  recovered. Proceed?" — you can add -f to skip the prompt: %reset -f
 
  This does not unload imported modules/functions the way Matlab's
  clear functions does — Python's import caching is a different
  mechanism, and reliably "unimporting" something is genuinely awkward
  in Python (there's importlib.reload() for reloading a single module
  after you've edited it, but it doesn't recursively reload things
  that module depends on, and it's a known source of subtle bugs).
  
  Restart the kernel (Jupyter: Kernel → Restart, or the circular-arrow
  button; IPython console: usually a keyboard shortcut or menu option)
  is the actually reliable equivalent of "wipe everything, including
  modules, and start over" — this spins up a fresh Python process
  under the hood, same idea as rerunning a script.

Practical recommendation given where you are: since you're going to be
iterating on preprocessing/PCA/model code a lot over the next while,
I'd suggest working in Jupyter (or JupyterLab, or a Jupyter-enabled
editor like VS Code's notebook support) rather than a plain script for
the exploratory phase — restart-kernel-and-run-all-cells becomes your
clear all, and it's a one-click/one-shortcut action, closer to the
Matlab habit you already have. Once the pipeline is solid, converting
to a plain .py script for actual training runs is easy.
